so for those of you who've been keeping  up with e3 you will have seen at the  lace installment of Assassin's Creed is  set in ancient Greece and is called  Odyssey a term that is often used to  describe a long live in full journey if  you are somewhat familiar of Greek  mythology then you also know that it's a  piece of work by the Greek poet Homer  which describes the tales and many  voyages of the Greek hero Odysseus after  the sack of Troy now this is quite  interesting because we are told that  Assassin's Creed Odyssey will take place  during the Peloponnesian War a war where  Athens and Sparta battled for control of  Greece a Battle of chaos and democracy  in this video I'll be recapping what was  mentioned by Ubisoft and making some  predictions on what we might see in the  game so in game you have a choice of two  characters either the male protagonist  Alexius or the female cassandra  whichever one of these two that you  choose it still seems that you played  the grandchild of the spartan king  leonidas with his broken spear being  your primary weapon now after seeing  what was done with Assassin's Creed  origins and their integration of the  Egyptian Pantheon I was eagerly awaiting  to see if they were delve into Greek  mythology with Odyssey and boy do they  not hold back on their references the  gameplay in cinematics that we were  shown were absolutely littered with  statues of heroes and gods ranging from  Zeus and Poseidon to Artemis and Athena  the character you play is even said to  bear the eagle of Zeus so I do think  that will be seen the Greek gods in some  shape or form which means for those of  you who have been craving more Greek  mythology since god of war or does he  may be the game for you now just based  on what we were shown at e3 it's very  difficult to gauge how the Greek gods  will feature my guess is that if we do  see the Greek gods it'll be quite  similar to the Trojan War where there  were gods on either side influencing the  outcome Athena being the patron deity of  Athens will of course side of the  Athenians perhaps even be in your  fiercest opposition now I'm sure quite a  few people would expect the patron god  of Sparta and the natural rival of  Athena to be Ares but the god that the  Spartans worshipped as their patron was  not actually a god but rather the  goddess Artemis which is why I believe  in the trailer we do see a fair amount  of animals being hunted  artemis of course was the goddess most  renowned for the hunt along with a giant  statue of autumn us we are showing one  more thing that may hint us in further  interaction with the goddess in one of  the traders that will be in shown we do  see some kind of stag or deer perhaps  being hunted we don't really get to see  this animal in too much detail because  of the Sun in the back but there is a  chance of this animals the ceranae and  her  for those of you who have somewhat  unfamiliar the ceranae in hind was a  huge and agile dear sacred to the  goddess Artemis and it's captures one of  the twelve labors of Heracles so I think  there is a chance as some kind of quest  or even a task to prove yourself to  Artemis that you'll be attempting to  capture this beast during the  Peloponnesian War itself both Athens and  Sparta made an appeal to the Persians  for help but none was given the Spartans  then chose to attack Persian occupied  states and eventually invaded Africa in  the year 431 BC which is when the game  is set so judging by what we've briefly  seen they're most likely be some kind of  Persian and African influences in the  game I'm not entirely sure if we're  going to see any of the heroes or  demigods as Heracles is mentioned and we  do see a statue of Perseus but I would  personally prefer to see some of the  lesser mentioned heroes such as  Bellerophon or even Jason I know like  myself a lot of people would have been  waiting to see the iconic creatures and  monsters and then it'd make us wait  right until the end when we see the back  of a creature that looks an awfully lot  like a Minotaur seeing the Minotaur is  definitely a good sign that there'll be  more monsters and creatures I'd be very  surprised if they just included the  Minotaur with so many great options  available in fact I do believe we get a  snapshot of what appears to be Medusa  which I expect is quite different to  what most people imagined we're told  much like Homer's Odyssey a portion of  this game we spent at sea so I expect to  either come across Plato's lost city of  Atlantis or maybe Scylla and many of the  other sea monsters and deities so now  you've heard some of my theories of what  we could possibly see in the game I  guess it's time for my overall opinion  and what we were shown I did see a lot  of people complaining about how Odyssey  looks like a reskin version of  Assassin's Creed origins and for the  most part I can see with some of these  people coming from if we're being honest  most people were going to play this game  because of the setting because of the  allure of Greek mythology and the  possibility of fighting against the  Greek pantheon one thing however that  did stick out to me was the accents that  we see in the game there are some fairly  okay Greek accents that we will shown  but there are also some really really  bad middle-eastern accents that are  meant to be Greek I guess and then we  have some British accents thrown in  there for good measure so I'm not sure  what they're going with but it does kind  of break the immersion of the whole game  overall I'm definitely excited to see  more of the game which creatures on  which gods that integrate into it and  how they approach Greek mythology as a  whole if you did manage to catch the  Assassin's Creed Odyssey reveal then let  me know how you felt about it  the only thing in particular that  excited you or was there something that  you just didn't like let me know in the  comments below  [Music]
 

